## Feature lists make buyers tune out

Decision-makers are flooded with messages that behave like static sales scripts instead of conversational triggers. They receive walls of text listing features, integrations, and software stacks. The words change, but every message makes the same vague promise.

> “Stop listing features and start breaking patterns.” Tejas Jhaveri, Founder of Myntmore

If you open with a generic claim about how your software “streamlines workflows,” the buyer’s brain tunes out. If you identify a specific point of friction and articulate the day-to-day problem better than the buyer can, you earn trust and curiosity.

## Know what the buyer is comparing

Strong pain articulation depends on understanding the alternatives. If you do not know the genuine strengths and gaps in a competitor’s offer, you will fall back on generic features and get blindsided when a prospect raises a comparison during the call.

Competitive positioning is not about attacking another company. It is about understanding where each option fits, then explaining why your approach is better suited to this buyer’s exact situation. If that positioning is not settled yet, it is the first thing we work out in a [GTM strategy engagement](/services/gtm-strategy).

## Stop improvising on sales calls

A useful battle card should give a representative the competitor’s positioning, publicly available pricing, documented strengths, recurring user complaints, and honest responses to likely objections. It should be specific to your offer, not a generic list of sales talking points.

## Research the pain before you write a word

You cannot articulate a buyer's problem better than they can if you have only read your own product page. The best material comes from three places most teams already have:

- **Sales and discovery calls.** Listen to recordings or reread call notes and write down the exact phrases buyers use to describe the problem. Their words, not yours, belong in your opening line.
- **Lost deals.** Every deal that went to a competitor or to doing nothing tells you which alternative the buyer trusted more, and why. Ask a few lost prospects for ten minutes; most will tell you more than a win ever does.
- **Public reviews.** Review sites and community threads about your competitors show what their customers love and what frustrates them. Recurring complaints are often the most honest description of the gap your offer fills.

Look for patterns, not single quotes. One buyer's complaint is an anecdote; the same complaint from five buyers is positioning.

## A battle card your team will actually use

A battle card earns its place on a live call only if a representative can read it in under a minute. Keep each card to one competitor and five short sections:

1. **Who they are best for.** An honest description of the buyer that competitor suits. Admitting this builds credibility.
2. **What buyers like about them.** Documented strengths, taken from reviews and lost deals, not from their marketing.
3. **Where buyers struggle.** The recurring complaints, in the customers' own words.
4. **Where we fit better.** The specific situations in which your approach is the better choice, tied to the pain you researched.
5. **Likely objections and honest answers.** The two or three questions prospects raise most when comparing you, with responses your team can say without exaggeration.

Update each card whenever a lost deal or a new review changes the picture. If you want a head start, the free [Competitor Battle Card Generator](/tools/battle-card-generator) researches a competitor's public positioning, pricing and reviews and drafts the card for you to check and refine.

## From feature pitch to pain articulation: an illustration

The difference is easiest to see side by side. This is an illustration, not a real campaign.

**Before:** "Our platform streamlines your reporting workflows with automated dashboards and 40 integrations."

**After:** "Most finance teams we speak to lose the first three days of every month rebuilding the same board report by hand. Is that still the case for you?"

The first line describes the product. The second describes a morning the buyer recognises, and asks a question they can answer in one word. It does not mention features at all, because a buyer who says yes will ask how it works.

## Positioning is a sales tool, not a slogan

Clear positioning makes every other part of outbound easier. Openers become specific, objections become predictable and calls stop drifting into feature comparisons. If the offer itself is the weak point, no amount of better wording will fix it; our post on [the offer problem](/resources/blogs/outbound-offer-problem) explains how to tell the difference.
